const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Predictive analytics","serviceType":"Data service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/predictive-analytics/","description":"Regression, classification, time-series and survival models for research, clinical and operational questions.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is predictive analytics, and what can it realistically do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It applies statistical modelling and machine learning to historical and current data to estimate the probability of future outcomes — which patients are at risk, how demand will move, what a trial is likely to recruit.\n\nWhat it cannot do is predict something with no precedent in the data, and it does not establish cause. A model tells you what tends to accompany an outcome, which is useful and is not the same as knowing why."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of model do you build?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Regression models including penalised forms, classification models such as trees, random forests and SVMs, time-series forecasting, survival analysis for censored outcomes, and clustering for stratification.\n\nThe choice follows the question rather than fashion. In clinical settings an interpretable model that performs within a small margin of a complex one is usually the better answer, because somebody has to defend it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you know a model actually works?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is tested on data it was not fitted to, and where possible on a later time period. That out-of-sample figure is the one reported.\n\nCalibration is reported alongside discrimination, because a model that ranks patients correctly but over-predicts risk at the top end will have you intervening with people who did not need it — and discrimination alone never shows that."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you support publication?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Reports are written to TRIPOD+AI or whichever standard applies, with the model specification, performance with intervals, calibration plot, and the code and environment needed to reproduce it.\n\nBuilding to the standard from the start means the same work supports an internal decision and a peer-reviewed paper without a second build."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this suitable for a small dataset or a small team?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, and the feasibility check is free precisely so you find out before committing. What matters is not the number of rows but the number of events — a large dataset with forty outcomes supports very little.\n\nWhere the data cannot carry the question, we say so. That answer is worth more than a model built anyway."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you deploy the model into our systems?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Integration and deployment are scoped separately, because a deployed model needs monitoring rather than delivering — performance drifts as the population and the recording practices change.\n\nWhere deployment is the goal, say so at the start. It changes what gets built, particularly around which predictors are available in production rather than only in the research extract."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/predictive-analytics/"}]}];

